    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "asgi.role-cross-cutting",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Use HTTP middleware only for cross-cutting concerns that must run on every request and response (security headers, compression, logging, metrics, request IDs, rate limiting); do not implement business logic in middleware.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "asgi.spec",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "ASGI (Asynchronous Server Gateway Interface) defines how async Python web applications communicate with servers, and middleware wraps ASGI applications to add behavior.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "asgi.callable-signature",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "ASGI middleware is an async callable that receives scope, receive, and send parameters and wraps an inner ASGI application.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "asgi.request-response-flow",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Middleware intercepts the request path before passing to the inner app, and intercepts the response path after the inner app completes, enabling pre-processing and post-processing.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "asgi.composability",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Multiple middleware can be composed by wrapping one middleware around another, forming a stack where each layer adds its own behavior.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "middleware-stack-behavior",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Understanding middleware ordering and how the stack executes. | Middleware Stack Behavior",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "stack.ordering",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Middleware is applied as a stack; the last-added middleware becomes the outermost wrapper, so it runs first on the request path and last on the response path.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "stack.flow-example",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "If MiddlewareA is added first and MiddlewareB second, the request flow is MiddlewareB \u2192 MiddlewareA \u2192 application, and the response flow is application \u2192 MiddlewareA \u2192 MiddlewareB.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "stack.ordering-critical",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Decide the desired request-path execution order first, then add inner middleware earlier and outer middleware later so critical concerns like security always run.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "stack.reasoning",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Understanding stack behavior is essential for debugging; trace through the stack mentally when issues arise with header manipulation, compression, or error handling.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "asgi-middleware-implementation",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Standard ASGI class pattern for implementing custom middleware. | ASGI Middleware Implementation Pattern",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "impl.class-structure",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Implement custom middleware as ASGI-compatible classes with __init__(self, app, **config) storing the inner ASGI app and configuration, and async __call__(self, scope, receive, send) wrapping the lifecycle.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "impl.scope-type-check",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Check scope[\"type\"] at the start of __call__ to handle only relevant request types (http, websocket) and pass through others unchanged.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "impl.call-inner-app",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Always call await self.app(scope, receive, send) to pass control to the inner application; skipping this breaks the middleware chain.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "impl.configuration",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Accept configuration via __init__ parameters rather than reading from globals or environment, allowing different configurations per middleware instance.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "impl.async-awareness",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "All middleware methods should be async; avoid blocking operations that would prevent the event loop from handling other requests.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "cross-cutting-concerns",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Guidance on what belongs in middleware versus application logic. | Cross-Cutting Concerns",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "concerns.appropriate",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Appropriate middleware concerns include logging, metrics, security headers, compression, request IDs, rate limiting, authentication header extraction, and CORS handling.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "concerns.inappropriate",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Do not implement business logic, domain-specific validation, or complex workflows in middleware; these belong in services and routers.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "concerns.global-coverage",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Cross-cutting concerns in middleware apply uniformly to all routes, including error handlers; this is their primary advantage over router-level logic.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "concerns.centralization",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Keep cross-cutting concern implementations centralized in middleware modules rather than duplicating logic across routers or services.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "concerns.thin-implementations",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Middleware implementations should be thin; complex logic should be delegated to dedicated modules that middleware calls.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.middleware-patterns.yml",
    "id": "errors.exception-safety",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Ensure middleware behavior is exception-safe; any resources acquired before calling the inner app must be released in finally blocks.",
    "splits": []
  },
